  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reference value is zero, as there is no sure outcome (100% probability) in either lottery. The outcomes in both lotteries are negative or zero, so the differences from the reference value (zero) are simply the outcomes themselves: -6000 for Lottery A (with 0.1% probability) and -3000 for Lottery B (with 0.2% probability). The mean difference for each lottery is calculated as the expected value of these differences: for A, it is (-6000) * 0.001 = -6; for B, it is (-3000) * 0.002 = -6. Thus, both lotteries have the same mean difference (-6 Euros). The decision reason would therefore be indifferent between A and B, as the mean differences are equal.",
