{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The problem does not contain a sure outcome (i.e., no outcome with 100% probability), so the reference value is zero, as specified. The reason evaluates the differences between each outcome and this reference value (zero), weighting them by their probabilities, and prefers the lottery with the more favorable (i.e., less negative, in this case) mean difference. For Lottery A: outcomes are -6000 (25%) and 0 (75%); differences from reference (0) are -6000 and 0, so the mean difference is (-6000 * 0.25) + (0 * 0.75) = -1500. For Lottery B: outcomes are -4000 (25%), -2000 (25%), and 0 (50%); differences are -4000, -2000, and 0, so the mean difference is (-4000 * 0.25) + (-2000 * 0.25) + (0 * 0.5) = -1000 - 500 = -1500. The mean differences are equal (-1500), so the reason would be indifferent between A and B.",
